Aim: Simulating Thrashing in Virtual Memory – Simulate and analyze thrashing using a page fault counter

Theory:
1. What is Thrashing?
Thrashing is a condition in an operating system in which the system spends more time handling page faults and transferring pages between main memory and secondary storage than executing processes.
It occurs when processes do not have enough frames to store the pages they frequently need.
2. What is Virtual Memory?
Virtual memory is a memory management technique that allows a computer to execute programs larger than its available physical memory. It uses secondary storage as an extension of main memory.
When a required page is not present in main memory, a page fault occurs, and the operating system loads the required page from secondary storage.
3. Important Terms:
Pages: List of pages requested by the program.
Frames: Stores pages currently in memory.
Capacity = 3: Memory can hold three pages.
Faults = 0: Counts the page faults.
If page not in frames: Checks whether the page is missing from memory.
Faults += 1: Increases the page fault counter.
Frames.append(page): Adds a page to memory.
Frames.pop(0): Removes the oldest page when memory is full.
Page Hit: The page is already in memory.
Page Fault: The page is not in memory and must be loaded.

Code:
#include <stdio.h>

int main()
{
    int pages[] = {1, 2, 3, 4, 1, 2, 5, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
    int n = 12, frames[3], capacity = 3;
    int faults = 0, i, j, found, pos = 0;

    for (i = 0; i < capacity; i++)
        frames[i] = -1;

    for (i = 0; i < n; i++)
    {
        found = 0;

        for (j = 0; j < capacity; j++)
        {
            if (frames[j] == pages[i])
                found = 1;
        }

        if (found == 0)
        {
            frames[pos] = pages[i];
            pos = (pos + 1) % capacity;
            faults++;

            printf("Page Fault: %d\n", pages[i]);
        }
        else
        {
            printf("Page Hit: %d\n", pages[i]);
        }
    }

    printf("Total Page Faults = %d\n", faults);

    if (faults > n / 2)
        printf("Thrashing may occur\n");
    else
        printf("Thrashing is not detected\n");

    return 0;
}



